* Some commands to manipulates ncurses windows.

Later we could have commands to add or remove windows, but for now we will assume
we have the four windows always open.

- stack
- source
- interactor
- repl

** display

Let's draw vertical separations with a column containing a single (configurable) | character.
It may be drawn in a specific face (foreground/background) (configurable).

For horizontal separations, we can use the "status line" (inspired from emacs), which is
a line containing the title of the window, and possibly other information.
The active window would have its status line highlighed (eg. INVERT (configurable) on the whole width of the windows).
The other windows would have their status lines dimmed (eg. UNDERLINE (configurable) on the whole width of the window).


At any time, there must be one active window.
When we enter commands, it goes to the active window.
The command may change the active window.

On the top-of-stack  of interactors we therefore have the ncurses
interactor, that catches windows manipulation commands, and pass down
the other interactors in the stack  the other commands.

** commands to select window

| keys  | description                               | command                |
|-------+-------------------------------------------+------------------------|
| C-w n | change the active window to the next.     | window-select-next     |
| C-w p | change the active window to the previous. | window-select-previous |

The windows are implicitely ordered from left to right and from top to
bottom, like the usual text flowing.

** commands to move windows

We don't assume 2x2, there may be more columns or rows of windows.

| keys  | description                                      | command               |
|-------+--------------------------------------------------+-----------------------|
| C-w > | Swap the active window with the one on the right | window-swap-right (1) |
| C-w < | Swap the active window with the one on the left  | window-swap-left  (2) |
| C-w u | Swap the active window with the one above        | window-swap-above (3) |
| C-w d | Swap the active window with the one below        | window-swap-below (4) |

(1) If the active window is already the right-most, then it swaps with the left-most one in the same row.
(2) If the active window is already the left-most, then it swaps with the right-most one in the same row.
(3) If the active window is already the top-most, then it swaps with the bottom-most one in the same column.
(4) If the active window is already the bottom-most, then it swaps with the top-most one in the same column.

Swapping windows change the implicit order.

** commands to change the window organization.

In the current system, we maintain the number of windows visible.


| keys  | description                                                               | command                  |
|-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------|
| C-w 2 | Creates a horizontal window below the row of the active window            | window-split-below (1)   |
| C-w 3 | Creates a vertical window on the right of the column of the active window | window-split-right (2)   |
| C-w 4 | Arranges the windows in a matrix closest to NxN                           | windows-split-square (3) |


Theory:

- we have a screen rectangle
- the screen rectangle can be split vertically or horizontally.
- the 2x2 disposition can be a vertical+horizontal or a horizontal+vertical split.

C-w 2 splits the current window horizontally
C-w 3 splits the current window vertically

But since splitting windows opens a new window and we maintain a constant number of window, we must kill one.
We kill the next window (remove its split) and move it to the new window (the new split).


C-w 4 reverts to the canonical 2x2 split: (horizontal (vertical 1 2) (vertical 3 4))



window tree = (horizontal above below) | (vertical left right)
with above or below being vertical, and
left or right being horizontal.
nodes = index | (active index)
There's always 1 and only 1 active index in the tree.
There's always 4 index in the tree.

C-w 2: replace active node by (horizontal (active index) next)

       tree = (… (<split> (active index) next) …)
       C-w 2
       tree = (… (horizontal (active index) next) …)
       
       tree = (… (<split1> (<split2> … (active index)) next) …)
       C-w 2
       tree = (… (<split 2> … (horizontal (active index) next)) …)


C-w 3: replace active node by (vertical (active index) next)

       tree = (… (<split> (active index) next) …)
       C-w 2
       tree = (… (vertical (active index) next) …)
       
       tree = (… (<split1> (<split2> … (active index)) next) …)
       C-w 2
       tree = (… (<split 2> … (vertical (active index) next)) …)
       
C-w 4: reverts to: (horizontal (vertical 1 2) (vertical 3 4)) with (active n) remaining (active n) 

  
Note: horizontal and vertical have arity 2, but (horizontal 1 (horizontal 2 3))
and (horizontal (horizontal 1 2) 3) are displayed the same:

#+begin_example
  1
-----
  2
-----
  3
-----  
#+end_example

So:

(vertical (horizontal (horizontal 1 2) 3) 4)

#+begin_example
  1  |
-----|
  2  |  4
-----|
  3  |
-----+-----  
#+end_example

(vertical 1 (horizontal (horizontal 2 3) 4))

#+begin_example
     |  2  
     |-----
  1  |  3  
     |-----
     |  4  
-----+-----  
#+end_example

** commands to change the window size

| keys  | description                                                             | command               |
|-------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------|
| C-w + | Increment active window size                                            | window-size-increment |
| C-w - | Decrement active window size                                            | window-size-decrement |
| C-w = | Give same size to active window and others in the same direction splits | window-size-balance   |


A prefix C-u [0-9]+  can be given to increment or decrement more than one row/column at a time.

At all times, the minimum window size is (/ screen-height number-of-windows)
rows of (/ screen-width number-of-windows) columns, so incrementing or
decrementing a window size may be impossible if this reaches these limits
for any window.

The size of the window is relative to its split.
Ie, with (vertical (active a) b)
window-size-increment increments the width of a and decrements the width of b.

Ie, with (vertical ? (vertical (active a) b) ?)
window-size-increment increments the width of a and decrements the width of ? when b has reached minimum width.

** command to save and load the window layout

| keys  | description                 | command               |
|-------+-----------------------------+-----------------------|
| C-w s | save current window layout  | window-layout-save    |
| C-w w | write current window layout | window-layout-save-as |
| C-w l | load current window layout  | window-layout-load    |
| C-w d | delete window layout        | window-layout-delete  |

Several window layouts can be saved into or loaded from the aldo
configuration, under a name given by the user.  C-w l displays the
list of window layout name saved, to let the user select one with
arrows or by typing the (start of the) name.

window-layout-delete asks for a saved layout like load, and ask to
confirm suppression.

window-layout-write asks for a name, and if already exists ask to
override.

window-layout-save does window-layout-write the first time (with
initial unsaved).

** minibuffer

The last line of the screen is not used by windows. It's named the minibuffer, and serves for command I/O:



| keys | description                                       | command                  |
|------+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------|
| M-x  | reads a command in the minibuffer and executes it | execute-extended-command |


M-x displays "M-x " in the minibuffer, and reads a command name (spaces may be used in place of dashes in command names).
RET executes the command. If the command takes parameters, it may prompt for them and read them from the minibuffer.

The user may define his own commands and bindings, and may bind predefined commands to his own keys.
The user binding map may shadow predefined bindings.

Lisp functions will be provided to let the user define the binding
maps, and invoke predefined commands, from his own commands.


